// src/kernel/boot/witness/r30_crash_isolation.pdx -- R30.M9-002 (#1086)
//
// The acpi-bubble crash isolation harness. Spawns the userspace
// acpi_supervisor binary as a boot-time service, hard-crashes it via
// sys_exit_body, and asserts that the WHOLE post-crash chain runs:
// capability sweep, driver row rebind, endpoint revival. The single
// end-of-boot marker "R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION OK" ships iff every
// sub-test observed its expected side effect. See the fingerprints
// block at end of file for why the marker spells "ACPI" rather than
// the upstream framework name.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this witness exists, and what it does NOT prove
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// R31.M2's #1600 and #1609 landed the two structural halves that make
// process crash isolation possible: (1) the respawn arm inside
// driver_death_notify rebinds a driver row to a fresh incarnation, and
// (2) driver_restart_revive_endpoints clears the ChannelDead latch on
// every endpoint the reap marked dead. R31Respawn.witness_r31_respawn
// exercised both halves against a synthetic driver row (slot 23) and a
// synthetic hard-coded endpoint id (23), which was enough to prove the
// primitives.
//
// This witness exercises the same primitives against the REAL image
// #1086 is filed against -- src/user/acpi_supervisor.pdx -- and the
// REAL endpoint id its sidecar pins (endpoint_id=3, see the
// acpi_supervisor.pdx:151 comment referenced from every step of the
// chain). The endpoint id 3 is the specific value stale-capability
// laundering would launder authority through, so a harness that
// substitutes a synthetic id for it can pass while the acpi_supervisor
// itself would still be broken. The single-line difference is where the
// value-under-test comes from -- the sidecar rather than a literal --
// and it is precisely the difference #1086 exists to close.
//
// This witness does NOT prove that ring-3 acpi_supervisor actually
// SERVES a client between kill and respawn -- the kill happens BEFORE
// sti, so the supervisor never reaches ring 3, and echo_client's own
// ring-3 RPC continues to serve as the substrate's live-conversation
// witness. What is proved here is the crash-side chain: sweep on the
// owner axis, rebind on the row axis, revive on the endpoint axis.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Slot and endpoint id
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Driver slot 24 continues the tally: 23 is r31_respawn.pdx's, 24 is
// the lowest single slot no other witness in the tree names. See
// design/drivers/witness-slot-plan.md for the running list.
//
// Endpoint id 3 is NOT chosen by this witness -- it is the value the
// acpi_supervisor.pdx sidecar carries (see acpi_supervisor.pdx:151),
// and the endpoint the loader will seed cap_slot 8 to point at.
// endpoint_alloc_at(3) must run BEFORE boot_spawn_user_task so the row
// exists when the sidecar seed lands (and before the driver_death_bind
// gate reads it); it must NOT run before r31_spawn's own alloc_at(1)
// and alloc_at(2) because those are earlier in the boot sequence, but
// r31_spawn has already returned by the time this witness runs, so
// there is nothing to race with.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why endpoint_bind_driver runs between the death-key bind and the kill
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Same argument as r31_respawn.pdx sub-test 12: driver_restart_reap_endpoints
// scans by driver_slot, not by pid. So the endpoint must be BOUND to
// slot 24 at the moment the reap runs, or the reap finds nothing to
// mark dead and the revive assertion below is vacuous. The bind runs
// AFTER driver_death_bind so the death correlation key is in place
// before any state that could observe it exists, and BEFORE the kill so
// the reap sees the bound row.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// runq_dequeue is load-bearing, for the same reason as r31_respawn
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sys_exit_body marks the killed task ZOMBIE and calls
// driver_death_notify -- but it does NOT dequeue the task from the
// runqueue, because in production every call site is a task exiting
// itself and the caller performs the dequeue-plus-pick-plus-switch
// tail. Here we invoke sys_exit_body from kernel_main against a task
// that is NOT _current_tcb, and no such tail runs. Without the dequeue
// the ZOMBIE lives on the runqueue and the next sched_pick_next_r15
// picks it, tripping the #663 guard on the next sched_block. See
// r31_respawn.pdx's header for the full trace.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fingerprint ordering
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sys_exit_body's tail emits "R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK count=N" via
// boot_spawn_note_death BEFORE this witness's own OK marker; the
// acpi_supervisor sidecar seeds 2 slots (KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT at 8,
// KIND_ACPI at 9), so N=2 for this death. The golden orders those two
// lines back-to-back so a sweep count of anything but 2 fails the
// sub-sequence match, matching the discipline r31_respawn established
// with count=1 for the echo_server death.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The push at entry
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Same alignment argument r31_respawn.pdx's header states verbatim:
// boot_continue_after_ring3 runs its body at rsp %16 == 8, so a bare
// call into this function would present rsp %16 == 0 to the body and
// every nested-call alignment argument in this file would invert. The
// push restores parity; the pop and ret restore the caller.

module R30CrashIsolation = structure {

  // witness_r30_crash_isolation() -- spawn acpi_supervisor, kill it,
  // observe the whole death cascade (sweep + rebind + revive) end-to-end
  // against the REAL hard-coded endpoint id the R30 bubble exists to
  // survive across.
  //
  // Effects and capabilities widened @{boot} -> @{boot, cap, sched} for
  // the same reasons as the enclosing boot_continue_after_ring3:
  // {cap} from the transitive cap_owner_sweep_revoke inside the death
  // path (and from boot_spawn_user_task's own seed), {sched} from
  // boot_spawn_user_task's runq_enqueue and this witness's runq_dequeue.
  pub let witness_r30_crash_isolation : () -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched} = fn () -> unsafe {
    effects: { mem, sysreg },
    capabilities: { cap, sched, boot },
    justification: "R30.M9-002 (#1086): acpica-bubble crash isolation harness. Spawns the userspace acpi_supervisor image (embedded at _acpi_supervisor_bin_start), binds its hard-coded endpoint id 3 to driver slot 24, hard-crashes the task via sys_exit_body with a synthetic fault status (FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE | EXC_GP=13), then observes the whole post-crash chain end-to-end. Sequence: (0) endpoint_alloc_at(3) BEFORE spawn -- the sidecar seed at loader_seed_caps time points cap_slot 8 at endpoint 3, and the row must exist for the driver_death_bind gate to succeed and for driver_restart_reap_endpoints to find something to mark dead; (1) driver_table_register slot 24 with a synthetic name; (2) driver_lifecycle_transition Init -> Running so the row is in a non-Init state whose return to Init post-respawn is a non-vacuous assertion; (3) boot_spawn_user_task(_acpi_supervisor_bin_start, len) -- the primitive #1590 introduced, which claims the sidecar's caps for this task's (pid, generation) and registers the service in _boot_spawn_pids/gens so boot_spawn_respawn can find it; (4) capture (old_pid, old_gen, incarnation-before) into .bss so the assertions read from a fixed source rather than from registers that must survive the sys_exit_body call; (5) endpoint_bind_driver(3, 24, 0) binds endpoint 3 to slot 24 (incarnation 0 matches the pre-death snapshot); (6) driver_death_bind(24, old_pid) installs the correlation key; (7) sys_exit_body(task_slab, FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE|13) hard-kills the task -- triggers cap_owner_sweep_revoke, boot_spawn_note_death emitting R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK count=2 (KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT at slot 8 + KIND_ACPI at slot 9), driver_death_find_slot -> 24, driver_table_unbind_pid, driver_restart_node (which force-stops, reaps endpoints marking id 3 dead, tears down), boot_spawn_respawn (rewriting registry row IN PLACE), driver_death_bind of the new pid, driver_restart_revive_endpoints (clearing endpoint 3's dead latch), EV_RESTART outcome=4; (8) runq_dequeue(task_slab) is load-bearing -- see header for the SCHD ASSERT exp=1 got=3 discussion; (9) assertions (a)..(f): row has a fresh key, new_pid != old_pid, driver_sup_incarnation == 1, state == Init, endpoint 3 revived (endpoint_is_dead == 0), registry key rewritten in place (old gone, new present); (10) emit R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION OK. Stage counter in .bss so a failure prints WHICH sub-test tripped via r30_crash_fail_msg + line KV. Register discipline: entry push rbp restores the rsp %16 == 8 parity boot_continue_after_ring3 hands off, matching r31_respawn.pdx's idiom.",
    block: {
      push rbp;

      // ---- Sub-test 0: allocate endpoint 3 (acpi_supervisor's id). ----
      //
      // acpi_supervisor.pdx:151 pins endpoint_id=3. The sidecar seed at
      // loader_seed_caps time will store target endpoint_id=3 into
      // cap_slot 8; the endpoint row must exist so a client of that cap
      // reaches an alive row, AND so endpoint_bind_driver below has a
      // row to bind. endpoint_alloc_at refuses a row that is already
      // taken, so this is unconditional at boot time -- r31_spawn
      // claimed 1 and 2, r31_respawn claimed 23, no witness claims 3.
      xor rax, rax;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;                              // stage = 0

      mov rdi, 3;                                  // svc.acpi endpoint id
      call endpoint_alloc_at;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // EP_ALLOC_AT_OK
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 1: register driver slot 24. ----
      //
      // pid argument is decorative -- the driver-row's pid field is NOT
      // the correlation key. Sub-test 6's driver_death_bind installs
      // the real key (pid, generation) in the row's pid_gen field. The
      // "42" mirrors r31_respawn.pdx's convention.
      mov rax, 1;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 24;                                 // slot
      mov rsi, 42;                                 // decorative pid
      lea rdx, [rip + _r30_crash_driver_name];
      xor rcx, rcx;                                // caps_manifest_offset
      call driver_table_register;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_TABLE_REGISTER_OK
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 2: drive the row Init -> Running. ----
      //
      // Not required for the cascade -- driver_restart_force_stop walks
      // Init -> Stopping -> Stopped via legal edges -- but required for
      // assertion (d) "state == Init post-respawn" to be non-vacuous
      // (a row still in Init at kill would read Init post-respawn even
      // if the re-arm had done nothing).
      mov rax, 2;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 24;
      mov rsi, 1;                                  // DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
      call driver_lifecycle_transition;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_LIFECYCLE_OK
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 3: spawn the userspace acpi_supervisor. ----
      //
      // boot_spawn_user_task creates a schedulable ring-3 task, seeds
      // its sidecar caps via loader_seed_caps (cap_slot 8 pointing at
      // endpoint 3, cap_slot 9 as KIND_ACPI) with cap_owner stamped to
      // this task's (pid, gen), and registers the service in
      // _boot_spawn_pids/gens so boot_spawn_respawn can find it at
      // death time. The task is on the runqueue but sti has not fired,
      // so the supervisor never reaches ring 3 before we kill it --
      // which is deliberate, the harness is structural.
      mov rax, 3;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rdi, [rip + _acpi_supervisor_bin_start];
      lea rax, [rip + _acpi_supervisor_bin_end];
      sub rax, rdi;
      mov rsi, rax;                                // image_len
      call boot_spawn_user_task;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je r30_crash_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_task_slab];
      mov [rcx], rax;                              // save the task slab

      // ---- Sub-test 4: snapshot (old_pid, old_gen, incarnation-before). ----
      //
      // Captured pre-kill because pid_gen_read after task_free would
      // resolve the wrong incarnation (pid_free does not reset
      // _pid_gen), and driver_sup_incarnation post-death would already
      // reflect the re-arm.
      mov rax, 4;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      // pid: u32 at task_slab+0.
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_task_slab];
      mov rax, [rax];
      xor rcx, rcx;
      mov ecx, [rax + 0];
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_old_pid];
      mov [rax], rcx;

      // generation: pid_gen_read(old_pid). Non-zero by construction --
      // the supervisor has not been reaped yet.
      mov rdi, rcx;
      call pid_gen_read;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r30_crash_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_old_gen];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      // incarnation-before: driver_sup_incarnation reads 0 on a row
      // that has never re-armed (the .bss default). Assertion (c)
      // below reads exactly 1 post-death.
      mov rdi, 24;
      call driver_sup_incarnation;
      cmp rax, 0;
      jne r30_crash_fail;                          // pre-death must be 0

      // ---- Sub-test 5: bind endpoint 3 to driver slot 24. ----
      //
      // driver_restart_reap_endpoints scans by driver_slot; the bind
      // must exist BEFORE the kill or the reap finds nothing to mark
      // dead and the revive assertion (e) below is vacuous. Incarnation
      // 0 matches the row's pre-re-arm state (the endpoint's own
      // incarnation field is NOT rewritten by the revive per
      // endpoint_dead_clear's justification).
      mov rax, 5;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 3;                                  // endpoint id
      mov rsi, 24;                                 // driver_slot
      xor rdx, rdx;                                // incarnation = 0
      call endpoint_bind_driver;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // EP_BIND_OK
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 6: bind the row's death key to the supervisor pid. ----
      //
      // driver_death_bind reads the generation out of the scheduler
      // itself; the supervisor is live at this moment so BIND_OK is
      // required. Without this, driver_death_find_slot in the death
      // path answers SLOT_NONE and the row-side chain never runs.
      mov rax, 6;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_old_pid];
      mov rsi, [rax];                              // pid
      mov rdi, 24;                                 // slot
      call driver_death_bind;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRV_DEATH_BIND_OK
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 7: kill the supervisor with a fault-status. ----
      //
      // FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE (0x100) | vector -- vector 13 is #GP,
      // matching the fault_kill.pdx production idiom. Status 0 would
      // have been a graceful exit; a fault status is what a REAL crash
      // in the ACPI bubble would produce (say, a null-pointer inside
      // an AML method), and this witness exists precisely to prove
      // that the crash chain runs against the shape of a real death.
      //
      // sys_exit_body's chain from here: mark ZOMBIE, wake parent,
      // cap_owner_sweep_revoke (2 slots -> emits R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK
      // count=2), driver_death_notify -> find_slot 24, unbind_pid,
      // audit EV_RESTART outcome=2, driver_restart_node (force_stop +
      // reap_endpoints marking id 3 dead + teardown), boot_spawn_respawn
      // (rewrite registry row in place, return new pid),
      // driver_death_bind with new pid, driver_restart_revive_endpoints
      // (clear endpoint 3's dead latch), audit EV_RESTART outcome=4.
      mov rax, 7;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_task_slab];
      mov rdi, [rax];                              // current = supervisor slab
      mov rsi, 0x10D;                              // FAULT_KILL_STATUS_BASE | 13
      call sys_exit_body;

      // ---- Sub-test 8: runq_dequeue the freshly-ZOMBIE supervisor. ----
      //
      // Load-bearing (see header for the SCHD ASSERT exp=1 got=3
      // story). runq_dequeue tolerates a task already off the runqueue,
      // but the supervisor is on it at this point, so the two-store
      // unlink fires.
      mov rax, 8;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_task_slab];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      call runq_dequeue;

      // ---- Sub-test 9 (a): row rebound to non-zero death key. ----
      //
      // driver_table_read_pid_gen returns the row's DEATH correlation
      // key (0 if unbound). Non-zero means driver_death_bind fired in
      // the respawn arm -- if zero, either the respawn refused or the
      // subsequent bind failed.
      mov rax, 9;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 24;
      call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r30_crash_fail;

      // Capture the rebound pid for assertions (b) and (f).
      mov rdi, 24;
      call driver_table_read_pid;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r30_crash_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_new_pid];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      // ---- Sub-test 10 (b): new pid != old pid. ----
      //
      // task_new uses pid_alloc's dense-low-first scan; the old pid
      // slot is still occupied by the ZOMBIE supervisor (parent_pid 0
      // by design, no reap), so the fresh pid cannot recycle the same
      // number.
      mov rax, 10;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_old_pid];
      mov rcx, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_new_pid];
      mov rdx, [rax];
      cmp rdx, rcx;
      je  r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 11 (c): driver_sup_incarnation(24) == 1. ----
      //
      // driver_table_rearm_incarnation increments the counter as its
      // penultimate step. A row that has never re-armed reads 0; one
      // that re-armed once reads 1. Any other reading is a silent
      // early-fail or a double re-arm.
      mov rax, 11;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 24;
      call driver_sup_incarnation;
      cmp rax, 1;
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 12 (d): state == Init. ----
      //
      // driver_table_rearm_incarnation zeroes the state byte as part
      // of its commit, taking the row from Stopped back to Init. This
      // is the observable that says the row is ready for a fresh
      // Running transition against a real driver lifecycle.
      mov rax, 12;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 24;
      call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_STATE_INIT
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 13 (e): endpoint 3 revived. ----
      //
      // THE #1086 LOAD-BEARING ASSERTION against the REAL hard-coded
      // endpoint id. endpoint_is_dead is exactly the predicate
      // sys_ipc_recv_body's ChannelDead gate consults: 0 means a
      // client's recv reaches user_bounce_recv instead of tripping
      // -ECONNRESET, i.e. the acpi_supervisor's respawn actually
      // recovered service continuity on the endpoint id its sidecar
      // pinned. A non-zero here means driver_restart_revive_endpoints
      // did not clear the dead latch for slot 24 -- the exact
      // regression this issue exists to gate.
      mov rax, 13;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 3;                                  // endpoint id
      call endpoint_is_dead;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // dead latch must be CLEAR
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 14 (f): registry rewritten in place. ----
      //
      // boot_spawn_respawn rewrites the SAME row rather than appending
      // (the four-row registry would exhaust otherwise). Assert BOTH
      // halves: OLD (pid, gen) must resolve to NO row; NEW (pid, gen)
      // must resolve to SOME row. A "just rebind" implementation that
      // appended would pass every other check on this list and quietly
      // leak rows.
      mov rax, 14;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_old_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_old_gen];
      mov rsi, [rax];
      call boot_spawn_is_service;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // OLD must be gone
      jne r30_crash_fail;

      // NEW half: read the fresh generation for the rebound pid.
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_new_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      call pid_gen_read;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r30_crash_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r30_crash_new_gen];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_new_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_new_gen];
      mov rsi, [rax];
      call boot_spawn_is_service;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // NEW must be present
      je  r30_crash_fail;

      // ---- Success: emit the marker. ----
      //
      // No count KV: the sweep count from boot_spawn_note_death was
      // already emitted as "R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK count=2" during
      // sys_exit_body above, and the golden pins that pair
      // back-to-back. A second count here would duplicate the claim.
      mov rdi, 3;                                  // LEVEL_INFO
      lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
      lea rdx, [rip + r30_crash_ok_msg];
      call klog_s1;
      jmp r30_crash_done;

      r30_crash_fail:
      // Same shape as r31_respawn.pdx's fail path: WHICH sub-test
      // tripped, as a decimal KV. Deliberately non-fatal: the boot
      // continues into ring-3 so a broken crash-isolation harness is
      // diagnosed from init's wire output rather than by a silent hang.
      lea rax, [rip + _r30_crash_stage];
      mov r8, [rax];
      mov rdi, 1;                                  // LEVEL_ERROR
      lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
      lea rdx, [rip + r30_crash_fail_msg];
      lea rcx, [rip + k_line];
      call klog_s1_d1;

      r30_crash_done:
      pop rbp;
      ret
    }
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Witness storage
  //
  // Every value that outlives a nested call site is in .bss rather than
  // in a register. Matches r31_respawn.pdx's discipline verbatim:
  // sys_exit_body's call chain does not preserve callee-save for us
  // across module boundaries in a way any compiler check enforces, so
  // a witness that parked one of these in r12/r13/r14 would be one
  // future edit to a downstream module away from silently reading
  // garbage.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

  // 16-byte name driver_table_register copies verbatim into row 24.
  // "acpi-crash-wits" is 15 characters plus one NUL for a 16-byte
  // payload; driver_table_register reads exactly two qwords starting
  // at name_ptr, so the padding is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
  pub let _r30_crash_driver_name : [u8; 16] = "acpi-crash-wits\0"

  // Sub-test index, mirrored in the failure path as a decimal KV so a
  // failing boot pins WHICH assertion tripped rather than just knowing
  // one did.
  pub let mut _r30_crash_stage : u64 = 0

  // The task_slab pointer boot_spawn_user_task returned. Copied to
  // .bss so it survives the sys_exit_body call in a fixed slot rather
  // than in a callee-save register the call chain need not preserve.
  pub let mut _r30_crash_task_slab : u64 = 0

  // The (pid, generation) pair of the supervisor BEFORE death, captured
  // for assertion (f)'s "OLD must be gone" reading. Reading via
  // pid_gen_read AFTER the death would resolve the wrong incarnation
  // (the recycled slot -- pid_free does not reset _pid_gen), which is
  // why these are captured pre-kill.
  pub let mut _r30_crash_old_pid : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _r30_crash_old_gen : u64 = 0

  // The (pid, generation) pair of the RESPAWNED supervisor, read out
  // of the driver row AFTER the death path returned. new_pid comes
  // from driver_table_read_pid (which the respawn arm set via
  // driver_death_bind); new_gen comes from pid_gen_read(new_pid) at
  // assertion (f)'s NEW half.
  pub let mut _r30_crash_new_pid : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _r30_crash_new_gen : u64 = 0

  // Fingerprints. "MARKER" shape matches r31_respawn.pdx: no trailing
  // newline, klog_emit_core appends the KV list and the terminator
  // itself.
  //
  // The word "ACPICA" is banned in kernel string literals by
  // tools/lint-no-kernel-aml.sh (#822), so the fingerprint spells the
  // bubble as "ACPI" -- the R30 round's own naming (see witness/r30_platform.pdx
  // for the "R30 ACPI EVENT ROUTE OK" precedent) rather than adopting
  // the upstream project's name for the interpreter framework. The
  // ISSUE (#1086) title uses "acpica" as shorthand for the whole R30
  // bubble round; the LINT bans the identifier because there is no
  // dependency on that upstream, only a family resemblance.
  //
  // "R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION OK" is 27 chars + NUL = 28 bytes;
  // "R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION FAIL" is 29 chars + NUL = 30.
  pub let r30_crash_ok_msg   : [u8; 28] = "R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION OK\0"
  pub let r30_crash_fail_msg : [u8; 30] = "R30 ACPI CRASH ISOLATION FAIL\0"
}
